From: Alex Scotti <alexscotti@gmail.com>
Subject: vh265: a handle for a DV core that abandons a FEL enhancement layer

The Dolby Vision core can ask this decoder to stop feeding the enhancement
layer ("bypass MEL" in amdv.c, sent when the metadata parser returns MEL for an
RPU). Honouring it latches bypass_dvenl_enable, which drops the decoder from
DECODE_MODE_MULTI_DVBAL to plain streambase - and a streambase base layer never
reports HEVC_FIND_NEXT_DVEL_NAL, the only event that ever hands the shared core
to the enhancement decoder. The pair stops alternating permanently.

On a profile 7 FEL disc that is wrong and it costs the whole film. Measured on
John Wick 3 on an AM9 Pro: the enhancement decoder ready 118462 times and
scheduled 5, playback at 20.65fps with a repeated frame every ~400ms; with the
request ignored, 1469 scheduled against 40135 ready and 23.99fps with none.

The fix for that belongs where the request is decided, and amdv.c now declines
to send it for a stream the player has flagged FEL. This knob is OFF by default
and exists only as a handle for a DV core that still sends one - it is in a
module, so unlike the kernel-side fix it can be flipped on a running box.
---
--- a/drivers/frame_provider/decoder/h265/vh265.c
+++ b/drivers/frame_provider/decoder/h265/vh265.c
@@ -571,6 +571,15 @@
 static u32 dv_debug;
 
 static u32 force_bypass_dvenl;
+/*
+ * Ignore the Dolby Vision core's request to stop feeding the enhancement
+ * layer. See the event handler for why that request is wrong on a profile 7
+ * FEL Blu-ray. OFF by default: the decision belongs upstream of here, and
+ * amdv.c now declines to send the request at all for a stream the player has
+ * flagged FEL. This stays as a handle for a DV core that still sends it - set
+ * it to 1 and the enhancement layer survives regardless.
+ */
+static u32 dv_ignore_bypass_el;
 #endif
 #endif
 
@@ -9853,9 +9862,28 @@
 	else if (type & VFRAME_EVENT_RECEIVER_DOLBY_BYPASS_EL) {
 		if ((force_bypass_dvenl & 0x80000000) == 0) {
 			hevc_print(hevc, 0,
-			"%s: VFRAME_EVENT_RECEIVER_DOLBY_BYPASS_EL\n",
-			__func__);
-			hevc->bypass_dvenl_enable = 1;
+			"%s: VFRAME_EVENT_RECEIVER_DOLBY_BYPASS_EL%s\n",
+			__func__, dv_ignore_bypass_el ? " (ignored)" : "");
+			/*
+			 * Honouring this is not the cheap no-op it looks like:
+			 * it drops this decoder out of
+			 * DECODE_MODE_MULTI_DVBAL, and a streambase base layer
+			 * never reports HEVC_FIND_NEXT_DVEL_NAL - the only
+			 * event that ever hands the shared core to the
+			 * enhancement decoder. The pair stops alternating for
+			 * good and nothing clears the latch, which on a
+			 * profile 7 FEL title costs the whole film: measured on
+			 * John Wick 3, the enhancement decoder was ready 118462
+			 * times and scheduled 5, and playback ran at 20.65fps
+			 * with a repeated frame every ~400ms.
+			 *
+			 * amdv.c no longer sends the request for a stream the
+			 * player flagged FEL, which is the right place for the
+			 * decision. This override stays for a DV core that
+			 * still sends one.
+			 */
+			if (!dv_ignore_bypass_el)
+				hevc->bypass_dvenl_enable = 1;
 		}
 	}
 #endif
@@ -16963,6 +16991,9 @@
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(dv_debug, "\n dv_debug\n");
 
 module_param(force_bypass_dvenl, uint, 0664);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(dv_ignore_bypass_el,
+	"\n ignore the dv core's bypass-el request (wrong on profile 7 FEL)\n");
+module_param(dv_ignore_bypass_el, uint, 0664);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(force_bypass_dvenl, "\n force_bypass_dvenl\n");
 #endif
 
